            <ArticleTitle>Phrenic Nerve Injury After Radiofrequency Denervation of the Cervical Medial Branches.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>E42-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/papr.12398</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Radiofrequency denervation of the cervical medial branches is a possible treatment for chronic cervical facet pain syndrome when conservative management has failed. According to the literature, complications after radiofrequency denervation of the cervical medial branches are rare. We report a case of possible phrenic nerve injury after ipsilateral radiofrequency denervation of the cervical medial branches following a posterolateral approach.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2015 World Institute of Pain.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
